ee that the input is valid. Therefore, until\0A *          you have called psa_aead_verify() and it has returned #PSA_SUCCESS:\0A *          - Do not use the output in any way other than storing it in a\0A *            confidential location. If you take any action that depends\0A *            on the tentative decrypted data, this action will need to be\0A *            undone if the input turns out not to be valid. Furthermore,\0A *            if an adversary can observe that this action took place\0A *            (for example through timing), they may be able to use this\0A *            fact as an oracle to decrypt any message encrypted with the\0A *            same key.\0A *          - In particular, do not copy the output anywhere but to a\0A *            memory or storage space that you have exclusive access to.\0A *\0A * This function does not require the input to be aligned to any\0A * particular block boundary. If the implementation can only process\0A * a whole block at a time, it must consume all the input provided, but\0A * it may delay the end of the corresponding output until a subsequent\0A * call to psa_aead_update(), psa_aead_finish() or psa_aead_verify()\0A * provides sufficient input. The amount of data that can be delayed\0A * in this way is bounded by #PSA_AEAD_UPDATE_OUTPUT_SIZE.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation     Active AEAD operation.\0A * \5Cparam[in] input             Buffer containing the message fragment to\0A *                              encrypt or decrypt.\0A * \5Cparam input_length          Size of the \5Cp input buffer in bytes.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output           Buffer where the output is to be written.\0A * \5Cparam output_size           Size of the \5Cp output buffer in bytes.\0A *                              This must be appropriate for the selected\0A *                                algorithm and key:\0A *                                - A sufficient output size is\0A *                                  #PSA_AEAD_UPDATE_OUTPUT_SIZE(\5Cc key_type,\0A *                                  \5Cc alg, \5Cp input_length) where\0A *                                  \5Cc key_type is the type of key and \5Cc alg is\0A *                                  the algorithm that were used to set up the\0A *                                  operation.\0A *                                - #PSA_AEAD_UPDATE_OUTPUT_MAX_SIZE(\5Cp\0A *                                  input_length) evaluates to the maximum\0A *                                  output size of any supported AEAD\0A *                                  algorithm.\0A * \5Cparam[out] output_length    On success, the number of bytes\0A *                              that make up the returned output.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL\0A *         The size of the \5Cp output buffer is too small.\0A *         #PSA_AEAD_UPDATE_OUTPUT_SIZE(\5Cc key_type, \5Cc alg, \5Cp input_length) or\0A *         #PSA_AEAD_UPDATE_OUTPUT_MAX_SIZE(\5Cp input_length) can be used to\0A *         determine the required buffer size.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         The total length of input to psa_aead_update_ad() so far is\0A *         less than the additional data length that was previously\0A *         specified with psa_aead_set_lengths(), or\0A *         the total input length overflows the plaintext length that\0A *         was previously specified with psa_aead_set_lengths().\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be active, have a nonce\0A *         set, and have lengths set if required by the algorithm), or the